IS-demand-shortfall branch; 0.62 x 0.24 = 0.149 -> D = 15. Step 4, B is the residual: 100 - 7 - 28 - 15 = 50. SUM = 100. CONSISTENCY CHECK I PERFORMED, EXPLICITLY. (i) Against my own CQ3: P50 = 1.5% is far below A's 2.6% trigger, so A receives only right-tail mass; 7 points is the P(g >= 2.6) implied by my own stated P90 of 2.5, and putting any substantial mass on A would contradict my CQ3 — the lock's worked example (P50 of 2.1% forbidding most mass on A) applies to me a fortiori at 1.5%. (ii) Against my own CQ2: my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal of 0.55 for OECD core >=2.50% over 2027-36 maps, on the rising profile, to a 2032-36 central of 2.55%, which places 36% above C's 2.7% trigger; multiplied by P(g<2.0) = 0.78 that yields 28 for C, so C is derived from my CQ2 rather than asserted. If I had instead used CQ2's unconditional 10-yr 2.54% with its tighter sigma 0.35, P(core>=2.7) would be 0.33 and C would be 26 — within rounding, so the answer is not sensitive to that choice. (iii) D's 15 is consistent with AI-Q4 at 66%: D needs not just the impairment event but for its deflationary digestion to still be suppressing both growth and inflation in 2032-36 rather than in 2028-31, which is roughly a one-in-four conditional. A FINDING ABOUT THE TAXONOMY, which the lock forces into the open: my modal outcome is B at 50 points, but it lands in B as the RESIDUAL at a growth rate of ~1.5%/yr, BELOW B's own stated 1.7-2.5% band, routed there because inflation sits between 2.0% and 2.7% and so triggers neither C nor D. Substantively that is still a bifurcated barbell — physical/care scarcity and hyperscaler rents coexisting with white-collar compression and steep real term premia, exactly B's qualitative description — but anyone reading B as \"growth 1.7-2.5%\" will misread my distribution. Half my probability mass sits in a cell whose label implies ~1 pp more growth than I forecast.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17: